WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:05.250
Hi, Steve Kaufmann here again,
and today I'm going to talk about

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:10.560
whether pitch accent is important
in learning languages like Japanese.

00:00:11.310 --> 00:00:14.580
Remember if you enjoy these
videos, please subscribe, click

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:16.079
on the bell for notifications.

00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:19.280
So, first of all, I'm back home.

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:20.000
All right.

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:22.760
Uh, I was in Palm Springs for six weeks.

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:25.100
I went there for a variety of reasons.

00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:27.400
First of all, our house needed,
our house down there needed

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:28.610
some work to be done on it.

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:32.640
Uh, also, we were doing some
work here, so it was very messy,

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:34.560
drywall dust and so forth.

00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:38.820
So we went down there and we also
got our second dose of the vaccine.

00:00:39.330 --> 00:00:44.120
And then Friday night we came back
home and I'll go, I'll explain that

00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:47.180
a little bit later, but pitch accent.

00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:52.340
So there's quite a debate about pitch
accent, particularly in Japanese.

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:57.660
And I will post the link in the
description box to a person who speaks

00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:01.020
flawless Japanese, like a Japanese person.

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:04.020
And he is a proponent of
studying pitch accent.

00:01:04.020 --> 00:01:10.230
His name is Dogan, and I will also show
you a link to a video by another person

00:01:10.230 --> 00:01:14.580
whose name escapes me, who says that
pitch accent is not worth worrying about.

00:01:15.870 --> 00:01:19.110
And I will leave a third
link to a description of

00:01:19.110 --> 00:01:20.550
pitch accent from Wikipedia.

00:01:21.330 --> 00:01:27.660
So pitch accent is, uh, and it
doesn't just exist in Japanese.

00:01:27.690 --> 00:01:32.340
It's the idea that in languages
we have varying intonation.

00:01:32.850 --> 00:01:33.390
All right.

00:01:33.390 --> 00:01:38.009
Intonation, that's not necessarily
tied to the meaning of a word.

00:01:38.009 --> 00:01:38.340
So.

00:01:39.015 --> 00:01:44.295
In Chinese, if you say a Chinese
word with the wrong tone, it's

00:01:44.295 --> 00:01:46.155
a different meaning altogether.

00:01:46.485 --> 00:01:50.595
And people can be confused or
have difficulty understanding

00:01:50.595 --> 00:01:53.595
you if you use the wrong tones.

00:01:53.805 --> 00:01:56.775
That is not the case in Japanese.

00:01:57.660 --> 00:02:02.880
Other, uh, languages, which
according to Wikipedia have this,

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:09.250
uh, pitch accent include Swedish,
Turkish, Persian, Western Basque.

00:02:09.350 --> 00:02:10.660
Okay, just Western  Basque.

00:02:11.310 --> 00:02:13.410
Uh, and I can't remember how...

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:19.590
ancient Greek a number of languages that,
um, according to them, Serbo-Croation

00:02:19.950 --> 00:02:22.320
have this pitch accent issue.

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:28.665
Now, let me say upfront that I lived in
Japan for nine years, I spoke Japanese,

00:02:28.665 --> 00:02:31.275
Japanese and still do quite comfortably.

00:02:31.605 --> 00:02:35.295
I met a lot of foreigners
who spoke Japanese very well.

00:02:36.315 --> 00:02:39.915
No one that I was aware of had
ever heard of pitch accent.

00:02:40.605 --> 00:02:43.605
So probably it exists.

00:02:44.205 --> 00:02:50.145
Maybe it's something that some people want
to spend time on, but it is not necessary.

00:02:50.265 --> 00:02:51.735
It is not necessary.

00:02:52.304 --> 00:02:56.505
It's not a unnecessary part of learning
the language, of communicating.

00:02:56.505 --> 00:03:00.674
It's certainly not necessary in so
far as comprehension is concerned.

00:03:01.394 --> 00:03:06.945
Um, in Chinese, on the other hand, if you
speak Chinese with very bad tones, people

00:03:06.945 --> 00:03:08.834
may have difficulty understanding you.

00:03:09.225 --> 00:03:11.834
And in any case, it sounds quite bad.

00:03:12.494 --> 00:03:15.225
It sounds as if you don't speak the
language very well, you would have

00:03:15.225 --> 00:03:20.535
to get those tones up to about 70,
80% accuracy to be taken seriously.

00:03:20.895 --> 00:03:23.204
In Japanese, that is not the case.

00:03:23.805 --> 00:03:26.835
I never pay any attention to pitch accent.

00:03:26.895 --> 00:03:31.875
I just consider, whatever it pitch accent
is and I still don't understand it, it's

00:03:31.875 --> 00:03:34.875
part of imitating the way people speak.

00:03:35.175 --> 00:03:37.875
Every language has its own intonation.

00:03:38.115 --> 00:03:42.495
Even within Japan, the intonation
in the Kansai and the Osaka area

00:03:42.495 --> 00:03:43.875
is different from the intonation.

00:03:44.519 --> 00:03:49.109
In, um, you know, the Northeast
and Tohoku area and the same

00:03:49.109 --> 00:03:50.339
is true for every language.

00:03:50.429 --> 00:03:53.970
I mean, if you've ever heard a French
Canadian, the Quebecois speak French,

00:03:54.209 --> 00:03:58.320
their intonation is very different
from the intonation in Paris,

00:03:58.530 --> 00:04:01.350
which is different from the
intonation in Southern France.

00:04:01.769 --> 00:04:06.870
Uh, if you've ever heard a Swiss
person speak, even English very often,

00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:08.519
they'll have their Swiss intonation.

00:04:08.519 --> 00:04:11.690
And I can only imagine that
their Hochdeutsch, their

00:04:11.690 --> 00:04:13.459
standard German also sounds

00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:18.630
quite different from the intonation
of a, of an Austrian or a

00:04:18.630 --> 00:04:20.730
Bavarian or someone from Hamburg.

00:04:20.730 --> 00:04:24.270
So the idea that there is
intonation in a language, I mean,

00:04:24.270 --> 00:04:25.740
we have intonation in English.

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:31.860
I remember when I, one of the reasons
why I decided to learn Cantonese was

00:04:31.860 --> 00:04:35.670
that having learned Mandarin with
its four tones and having all these

00:04:35.670 --> 00:04:38.370
Cantonese people scare you off saying

00:04:38.775 --> 00:04:42.235
cantonese is the most difficult
language because it has nine tones.

00:04:42.255 --> 00:04:46.545
And I said, geez, I mean, I had enough
trouble learning four tones, nine tones

00:04:46.545 --> 00:04:50.265
is just too difficult, but I found
this book written by a Chinese person,

00:04:50.265 --> 00:04:54.075
Cantonese speaker, who, first of all said,
you only need six tones for Cantonese.

00:04:54.375 --> 00:04:58.545
And second of all, pointed out that
we have tones in English and you

00:04:58.545 --> 00:05:01.905
can hear me hear me in my English.

00:05:02.085 --> 00:05:04.065
I also have tones.

00:05:04.395 --> 00:05:10.860
So, is it worthwhile, you know,
going into great detail, classifying?

00:05:10.860 --> 00:05:14.460
I mean, Persian is described
or Turkish as a tonal language.

00:05:14.610 --> 00:05:17.160
I just listened to it
and try to imitate it.

00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:19.260
Um, I think I do.

00:05:19.260 --> 00:05:19.650
Okay.

00:05:19.650 --> 00:05:22.530
I don't have enough vocabulary
in those languages to speak them

00:05:22.530 --> 00:05:25.500
well, but it's not my lack of

00:05:25.935 --> 00:05:29.025
emphasis on pitch accent
that's holding me back.

00:05:29.675 --> 00:05:36.525
Uh, by the way, just to digress, so my
wife and I head back home to Canada, uh,

00:05:36.945 --> 00:05:41.025
some trepidation about what's going to
happen at the border because actually

00:05:41.025 --> 00:05:45.945
the government heavily discourages you
from traveling outside the country.

00:05:46.395 --> 00:05:51.525
And if you fly into a major airport, you
have to quarantine in a hotel that the

00:05:51.525 --> 00:05:54.284
government dictates and you pay for it.

00:05:55.035 --> 00:05:59.715
While waiting for your first test results
to come back could be two or three days.

00:06:01.035 --> 00:06:02.025
We didn't want to do that.

00:06:02.385 --> 00:06:05.595
So we flew from Palm Springs
to Bellingham, which is 20

00:06:05.595 --> 00:06:06.615
minutes from the border.

00:06:08.025 --> 00:06:09.495
The luggage took a while to come out.

00:06:09.495 --> 00:06:12.885
So it was getting later and later
we had trouble getting an Uber.

00:06:12.885 --> 00:06:13.785
We couldn't get an Uber.

00:06:13.785 --> 00:06:16.890
We finally got a Lyft driver
to come to the airport.

00:06:16.890 --> 00:06:17.940
He drove us to the border.

00:06:17.950 --> 00:06:18.960
Now it's dark.

00:06:19.320 --> 00:06:20.550
It's close to 10 o'clock.

00:06:20.550 --> 00:06:23.969
We're dropped off at the Peace Arch Park.

00:06:24.270 --> 00:06:25.230
We don't know where to go.

00:06:25.230 --> 00:06:27.000
We wander around in the park.

00:06:27.030 --> 00:06:31.140
Finally found a sort of a sidewalk,
followed it, got to a road.

00:06:31.635 --> 00:06:35.685
Walked for about a mile, pulling my
luggage until we got to the border.

00:06:35.955 --> 00:06:38.715
And there, there, there's a very
serious looking sign there with a

00:06:38.715 --> 00:06:44.025
big stop sign stop until you are
"motioned forward by an agent".

00:06:44.235 --> 00:06:44.475
Okay.

00:06:44.475 --> 00:06:44.895
I stop.

00:06:45.105 --> 00:06:48.945
So we waited and waited and waited cars
every so often a car would come back and

00:06:48.945 --> 00:06:50.765
the customs official would come along.

00:06:50.985 --> 00:06:52.845
Customs official would deal with the car.

00:06:53.325 --> 00:06:55.125
I thought, how are they ever
going to pay attention to us?

00:06:55.335 --> 00:07:00.255
So finally they came and, um,
basically I had registered on a

00:07:00.255 --> 00:07:03.165
government app or downloaded an app.

00:07:03.165 --> 00:07:05.775
So I had already sent them
ahead all our information.

00:07:06.165 --> 00:07:08.595
So it went very, very smoothly.

00:07:08.595 --> 00:07:13.425
They knew who we were, uh, asked us a
few questions and then told us that we

00:07:13.425 --> 00:07:15.255
would have to quarantine for 14 days.

00:07:15.405 --> 00:07:19.935
And that the penalty for breaking
quarantine could be as high as a

00:07:19.935 --> 00:07:22.515
million dollars or three years in jail.

00:07:23.235 --> 00:07:26.205
I don't think they'd ever do that, but
that's kind of to scare you, right?

00:07:26.745 --> 00:07:31.965
And then they said, you're lucky because
the self-testing tent hasn't closed yet.

00:07:32.175 --> 00:07:35.805
So go off to the tent there and the
nurse, there will show you how to

00:07:35.925 --> 00:07:40.034
self test, because we're going to
have to self test again in seven days.

00:07:40.365 --> 00:07:44.025
So we went ahead, and there were two or
three different nurses that couldn't have

00:07:44.025 --> 00:07:47.265
been kinder, more helpful, more pleasant.

00:07:47.594 --> 00:07:51.495
Took us through our paces, registered
us on a website where we have to report.

00:07:51.975 --> 00:07:55.725
Then our second test, did
the test in our nostrils.

00:07:55.755 --> 00:08:01.095
One 15 seconds in each nostril, put it
in this little container, send it off.

00:08:01.095 --> 00:08:03.585
We're going to have to do
the same seven days later.

00:08:04.155 --> 00:08:10.305
Then we went out to find an Uber, uh,
walked a ways and then call the Uber.

00:08:10.495 --> 00:08:12.365
Our Uber driver got lost.

00:08:12.805 --> 00:08:16.784
ENded up actually having, I think he
went across into the United States.

00:08:16.935 --> 00:08:22.005
He then told us that the border
guards had told him that, uh, you

00:08:22.005 --> 00:08:24.255
know, he had to prove that he was
an Uber driver, so he wouldn't

00:08:24.255 --> 00:08:25.575
have to quarantine on his way back.

00:08:26.355 --> 00:08:28.935
Anyway, he was an African immigrant.

00:08:29.445 --> 00:08:32.625
So I was able to speak some
Farsi with him in the car.

00:08:33.155 --> 00:08:38.605
I have no idea what pitch accent he
had and I think it makes no difference.

00:08:39.095 --> 00:08:43.395
Just a brief aside, I think that the
only way we're getting through this,

00:08:43.815 --> 00:08:47.565
uh, COVID epidemic is through vaccines.

00:08:48.125 --> 00:08:52.155
Anybody who is not willing to get
a vaccine is simply a part of the

00:08:52.155 --> 00:08:53.925
problem, not a part of the solution.

00:08:54.314 --> 00:08:57.675
And the same was true with
masking before we were vaccinated.

00:08:57.675 --> 00:09:00.944
That was the only way we could
keep a lid on the pandemic.

00:09:01.035 --> 00:09:04.755
I just don't understand people who
in the name of some kind of freedom,

00:09:04.905 --> 00:09:11.204
refuse to cooperate with everybody else
in trying to basically help the whole

00:09:11.204 --> 00:09:12.495
of society get through this thing.

00:09:12.704 --> 00:09:13.865
Anyway, that's, as an aside...

00:09:13.915 --> 00:09:16.395
I have the freedom to drive through
a red light, it's the same idea.

00:09:17.025 --> 00:09:18.765
Anyway, getting back to pitch accent.

00:09:18.824 --> 00:09:19.305
So.

00:09:20.985 --> 00:09:23.415
I think all these languages
probably have pitch accent.

00:09:23.895 --> 00:09:29.115
I think there is a great tendency,
it's fine for linguists academic link

00:09:29.145 --> 00:09:33.705
with, to study different qualities
of languages and categorize them.

00:09:33.705 --> 00:09:38.415
And this is subject-verb-object, and
this is subject-object-verb, and this

00:09:38.415 --> 00:09:42.345
belongs to this other group and it
doesn't matter for the language learner.

00:09:42.405 --> 00:09:45.645
We simply have to imitate what we hear.

00:09:45.855 --> 00:09:51.660
We have to get used to the pattern
of the language and, um, In my

00:09:51.660 --> 00:09:53.550
Swedish, there are tones in Swedish.

00:09:54.140 --> 00:09:57.810
I like to Imitate ...you imitate it.

00:09:58.129 --> 00:10:00.109
Yeah, the same is true in Japanese.

00:10:00.380 --> 00:10:05.780
And if I spend a lot of time with
people from Osaka, uh, I end up

00:10:05.839 --> 00:10:08.120
with more of an Osaka accent.

00:10:08.689 --> 00:10:11.089
Um, it doesn't affect the meaning.

00:10:11.089 --> 00:10:12.999
It's not like Chinese tones.

00:10:13.515 --> 00:10:17.355
But it's something that, uh, again,
you know, you talk about tones that

00:10:17.355 --> 00:10:22.545
compare, uh, compare a Brazilian to
someone from Portugal, different area,

00:10:22.555 --> 00:10:25.665
different people, even native speaker,
they're going to have different types of

00:10:25.665 --> 00:10:32.175
intonation and you will naturally want
to imitate the accent and the intonation.

00:10:32.490 --> 00:10:36.450
That, that either you like the
best, or that is more useful for you

00:10:36.600 --> 00:10:39.960
in your work or you consider more
prestigious or whatever it might be.

00:10:40.530 --> 00:10:45.210
And I don't think, and the other thing
about pitch accent, it is possible that

00:10:45.210 --> 00:10:49.395
if I put a lot of effort on pitch accent,
so I decided, uh, I'm not going to be, I'm

00:10:49.395 --> 00:10:54.045
not going to allow myself to be influenced
by having spent two evenings with people

00:10:54.045 --> 00:10:58.905
from Osaka, which inevitably makes me
speak with more of an Osaka accent.

00:10:59.295 --> 00:11:04.215
I'm going to try to, you know,
nail down the, the sort of pitch

00:11:04.245 --> 00:11:09.285
accent of the Japanese national
broadcast announcer NHK announcer.

00:11:09.585 --> 00:11:12.555
So I'm gonna go whole hog
with the Dogan program.

00:11:13.590 --> 00:11:18.360
Uh, and, but the problem is that
language is about communication.

00:11:18.420 --> 00:11:21.060
It's not a performance sport.

00:11:21.240 --> 00:11:25.650
No one, I I'm not, I don't care if
people tell me, well, you're Japanese

00:11:25.650 --> 00:11:29.040
pronunciation is, sounds a bit
this way or that way I don't care.

00:11:29.490 --> 00:11:32.550
Uh, people are comfortable
communicating with me.

00:11:32.910 --> 00:11:36.480
I'm comfortable because I understand
what people are saying to me in Japanese.

00:11:37.190 --> 00:11:40.370
Um, I'm not seeking perfection.

00:11:40.755 --> 00:11:45.615
Uh, you know, it's, it's not a
performance it's it's communication.

00:11:45.825 --> 00:11:51.585
And to that extent people who do want
to achieve the NHK announcers, uh,

00:11:51.615 --> 00:11:58.785
intonation by all means, but to pretend
that this is important, uh, it's not as

00:11:58.785 --> 00:12:01.635
important as increasing your vocabulary.

00:12:02.025 --> 00:12:07.020
It's not as important as having a
good command of the language and

00:12:07.020 --> 00:12:10.470
using words appropriately, using
words that belong together, being

00:12:10.470 --> 00:12:11.880
able to express your thoughts.

00:12:11.910 --> 00:12:18.390
Clearly that's far more important than
pursuing the ultimate, you know, in tones.

00:12:18.750 --> 00:12:19.320
I ha...

00:12:19.350 --> 00:12:22.740
I mentioned this before, I had
a Swiss banker who came in with

00:12:22.740 --> 00:12:27.510
his sidekick from England, uh,
to try and sell something to us.

00:12:27.990 --> 00:12:30.720
The Swiss banker had a strong
Swiss accent in his English.

00:12:31.200 --> 00:12:36.120
But he used words so well, he expressed
himself so accurately, so elegantly

00:12:36.630 --> 00:12:41.340
and his English sidekick, maybe he
wasn't that well educated, he didn't

00:12:41.340 --> 00:12:45.240
use the English language as well,
but he spoke with a native accent.

00:12:45.480 --> 00:12:49.560
So if you want to pursue pitch
accent by all means do so.

00:12:49.560 --> 00:12:53.670
But in my opinion, it's not
that important and I certainly

00:12:53.670 --> 00:12:55.740
pay no attention to it anyway.

00:12:56.190 --> 00:12:59.685
Uh, and I'm going to leave a couple
of videos here to follow up on

00:12:59.685 --> 00:13:03.375
the subject of pronunciation and
seeking to be perfect and so forth.

00:13:03.405 --> 00:13:04.515
So thank you for listening.

00:13:04.814 --> 00:13:05.324
Bye for now.

